{
  "note": "Re-judged 2026-10-31 at HEAD 137c2dcfcf by an auditor who did not author dfe4e3aa8d or e2d9c4a62a. RFC 3948 Section 5.1: \"Because SGW will now see two possible SAs that lead to 10.1.2.3, it can become confused about where to send packets coming from Suzy's server.  Implementors MUST devise ways of preventing this from occurring.\" Ze's way is the cross-peer inner-selector registry (dropForeignInnerOverlaps at narrowing, innerSelectorRegistry.beginInstall under one lock at install): a pair one packet could match together with another peer's live pair, tunnel against tunnel or tunnel against transport, is refused TS_UNACCEPTABLE unless both pairs are operator-fixed and their policy-priority values differ; two transport-mode pairs are left to RFC3948-5.2-1's dataplane policyOwners.claim. dfe4e3aa8d staled five units by unparam rewrites only: orderedTransportPeers hardwires priorityB 1000, mustNamePeersAndFix hardwires tunnel peer a, orderedTransportChild drops its unused *ChildSA; every caller at HEAD passed those values, so inputs, priorities and asserted refusal strings are unchanged. Re-probed at HEAD by go -overlay (tree untouched; foreign in-flight edits to config/loader_redistribute.go and redistribute/destination.go overlaid with their HEAD blobs so the package built): isConfiguredHost accepting any host reddens TestRFC3948OrderedTransportLocalHalfNarrowedRefused and TestRFC3948OrderedTransportNarrowedToPortRefused only; operatorOrdered ignoring priority reddens TestRFC3948OrderedTransportEqualPriorityRefused only; operatorOrdered always false reddens the positive TestRFC3948OrderedTransportBesideFullTunnelServed only; bothTransport true when either pair is transport reddens TestRFC3948TunnelAroundTransportPeerRefused (and the three refusals that need the tunnel-transport comparison). So each changed unit still isolates its rule, its refusal is asserted as TS_UNACCEPTABLE with the RFC 3948 Section 5.1 reason, nothing on the second peer's dataplane and one registry holding, and the positive is a conforming configured-and-ordered pair, not a different error. The producers moved under these units since the last verdict (e2d9c4a62a split claim into beginInstall/finishInstall); the probes above ran against the current producers, so the stale discrimination record of TestRFC3948TunnelAroundTransportPeerRefused does not void this judgement, though it still owes a fresh ./le rfc record. Units not changed by dfe4e3aa8d keep the 2026-10-27 judgement: lease guards, claim atomicity (concurrent installs), initiator wiring, IKE and Child SA rekey carriers, stranded records, and the three audit-gap edges (classifyInnerPair transport test, samePolicyPair local port and local prefix). Those edges enforce Ze's stated rule, stricter than the bare MUST needs, and assert nothing the RFC contradicts. Weak spot kept from the last verdict: replacing childPolicyPair's protocol intersection leaves every RFC3948-5.1-1 unit green; the installed intersection is proven by RFC4301-4.4.2-2's record on TestRFC4301MixedProtocolPairInstallsTheIntersection.",
